{"results":[{"url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12982-026-02814-0","final_url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12982-026-02814-0","title":"Bacterial profile and associated factors of face mask contamination among health care workers - Discover Public Health","description":"Background Extended and improper mask use may lead to increased bacterial contamination of the mask surface, highlighting the need to better understand patterns of microbial load and persistence on masks in clinical settings. Objective This study aims to investigate the bacterial contamination in face masks used by healthcare workers (HCWs), identify the predominant bacterial species, and evaluate factors contributing to contamination. Methodology A cross-sectional study was conducted among 400 HCWs in secondary and tertiary hospitals in a metropolitan Indian city. Data was collected through interviews on demographics and hygiene. Mask and indoor air samples were analysed using conventional cultures and the Automated VITEK-2 for identification. Results Bacterial contamination was found in 84.5% of masks and included strains of MRSA (1.5%) and MSSA (0.25%). While environmental samples mirrored mask flora, MRSA was found only on the inner mask surfaces, suggesting HCWs may be nasal carriers. Bacterial loads were highest in wards and lowest in the ICU, with significantly higher counts linked to poor hand hygiene. In Univariate analysis, factors such as higher age, gender, clinical setting of work, surgical mask, higher patient contact, working hours, hospital providing mask and duration of mask usage were found to be significantly associated with colony count. Multivariable quasi-Poisson regression analysis identified clinical setting of work (RR 1.69, CI 1.29–2.21), Female Gender (RR 0.79, CI 0.67–0.93), surgical mask (RR 1.30, CI 1.05–1.61), and hospital providing masks (RR 0.80, CI 0.66–0.98) as predictors for bacterial contamination. Conclusion The high prevalence of bacterial contamination, including MRSA on masks, highlights the need for strict handling protocols, frequent replacement, and further research into the potential for cross-contamination and hospital-acquired infections.","language":"en","text":"## Article summary\n\nAI generated\n\nExtended and improper use of face masks by healthcare workers can lead to significant bacterial contamination, raising concerns about infection control in clinical settings. This study found that 84.5% of masks worn by healthcare workers were contaminated, with notable strains such as MRSA present, underscoring the urgent need for improved mask hygiene practices and handling protocols to mitigate infection risks.\n\nThis is an AI-generated summary, check important information.\n\n## Abstract\n\n### Background\n\nExtended and improper mask use may lead to increased bacterial contamination of the mask surface, highlighting the need to better understand patterns of microbial load and persistence on masks in clinical settings.\n\n### Objective\n\nThis study aims to investigate the bacterial contamination in face masks used by healthcare workers (HCWs), identify the predominant bacterial species, and evaluate factors contributing to contamination.\n\n### Methodology\n\nA cross-sectional study was conducted among 400 HCWs in secondary and tertiary hospitals in a metropolitan Indian city. Data was collected through interviews on demographics and hygiene. Mask and indoor air samples were analysed using conventional cultures and the Automated VITEK-2 for identification.\n\n### Results\n\nBacterial contamination was found in 84.5% of masks and included strains of MRSA (1.5%) and MSSA (0.25%). While environmental samples mirrored mask flora, MRSA was found only on the inner mask surfaces, suggesting HCWs may be nasal carriers. Bacterial loads were highest in wards and lowest in the ICU, with significantly higher counts linked to poor hand hygiene. In Univariate analysis, factors such as higher age, gender, clinical setting of work, surgical mask, higher patient contact, working hours, hospital providing mask and duration of mask usage were found to be significantly associated with colony count. Multivariable quasi-Poisson regression analysis identified clinical setting of work (RR 1.69, CI 1.29–2.21), Female Gender (RR 0.79, CI 0.67–0.93), surgical mask (RR 1.30, CI 1.05–1.61), and hospital providing masks (RR 0.80, CI 0.66–0.98) as predictors for bacterial contamination.\n\n### Conclusion\n\nThe high prevalence of bacterial contamination, including MRSA on masks, highlights the need for strict handling protocols, frequent replacement, and further research into the potential for cross-contamination and hospital-acquired infections.\n\n### Similar content being viewed by others\n\nArticle\nOpen access\n26 June 2026\n\nArticle\nOpen access\n06 May 2026\n\nArticle\nOpen access\n07 March 2022\n\n### Explore related subjects\n\nDiscover the latest articles, books and news in related subjects, suggested using machine learning.\n\n## 1 Introduction\n\nMasks serve as a barrier to protect both healthcare workers (HCWs) and patients from airborne pathogens, including bacteria, viruses, and other microorganisms [18]. Nevertheless, extended and inappropriate mask use may lead to increased bacterial contamination on mask surfaces, underscoring the need to characterise the extent and patterns of such microbial contamination [26]. Ding et al. [5], Yousefimashouf [33]. Despite being primarily made for protection, masks can become a source of contamination because of continuous adjustments, frequent touching, and contact with different surfaces [22].\n\nUnlike viruses, which require living host cells for replication, many bacteria and fungi can survive and multiply on inanimate surfaces. According to the World Health Organisation, the effectiveness of masks is dependent on a number of factors, including hand hygiene, mask usage, setting, and disposal [3, 15, 24, 30]. Given that these masks come into close contact with the respiratory tract, extra caution should be used to maintain hygiene and avoid preventable complications due to improper mask usage. Lo et al. [19], Park [24] Recent studies have also highlighted the substantial bacterial burden in the masks of HCWs, including clinically significant bacteria such as *Staphylococcus aureus* and *Klebsiella pneumonia* [16, 22]. Another concern is the presence of antimicrobial-resistant bacteria in hospital environments, which may be detected on masks, particularly when mask use and hand hygiene practices are suboptimal [22].\n\nA recent study published in scientific reports has shown that 99% of the mask samples collected from community volunteers showed bacterial colonies [14, 24]. Despite the importance of this issue, limited research has been conducted to assess the extent and nature of bacterial contamination in masks worn by HCWs from developing country settings. Understanding the microbial load and diversity on used masks is essential for developing evidence-based guidelines to optimise mask usage, storage, and disposal practices. This study aims to investigate the bacterial contamination in face masks used by HCWs, identify the predominant bacterial species present, and evaluate factors contributing to microbial growth. By shedding light on this overlooked aspect of mask hygiene, the findings can help in devising strategies for monitoring and improving mask handling practices in healthcare environments, infection control protocols and enhancing occupational safety in healthcare environments.\n\n## 2 Methodology\n\nA cross-sectional study was conducted among HCWs in 7 selected secondary and tertiary care hospitals/medical colleges in a metropolitan city in Karnataka, India. Hospitals were included based on permission granted for study participation. All HCWs (doctors, nurses, and other staff involved in direct patient care) working in the selected hospitals who were using masks were eligible for inclusion. This study was a part of a bigger project exploring microbial contamination in a hospital setting.\n\nSince prior data from this specific setting was unavailable, assuming a prevalence of 50% bacterial contamination in masks and precision of 5% at a 95% confidence level, the sample size was calculated to be 384, which was later rounded off to 400. HCWs were selected from different clinical areas (OPD, ICU/Isolation unit, General ward, Emergency ward) from all participating hospitals. A structured interview schedule was developed, comprising: Sociodemographic profile, occupational details, and medical history. Details on usage of masks, such as the type of mask used, frequency of change, duration of the current mask use, and hand hygiene practices with respect to mask usage, were collected.\n\n### 2.1 Mask sample collection and microbiological analysis\n\nThe used mask was collected aseptically from each participant in a sterile zip-lock bag. After collecting, all samples were transported to the laboratory under cold chain conditions to preserve microbial viability. Each mask was processed for quantitative bacterial culture. Park et al. [24] For sampling of masks, the inside and outside area of the face mask was directly imprinted on trypticase soy agar. Bacterial presence in the working environment was assessed using a settle plate (i.e., the sedimentation or depositional method). Stanley et al. [29], Viani [31] Environmental samples were collected from 52 areas.Petri dishes with media were placed on the 4 corners and in the centre of the room, and allowed air to settle on solid culture media using the 1/1/1 Scheme (expose for 1 h, 1 mt above the ground,1 mt away from the wall). All the agar plates were immediately incubated at 35 °C ± 2 °C for 48 h for bacteria count. This 48-hour incubation period was selected in accordance with standard clinical microbiology protocols to ensure complete colony development of target aerobic pathogens without risking media desiccation. After incubation, bacterial colonies were counted, and results were expressed as colony-forming units per plate (cfu/plate) and the bacterial concentration in the indoor environment was calculated as described by Omeliansky’s equation [29, 31]. Colony morphology was studied, and each unique colony was sub-cultured to get a pure culture on routine media like Chocolate agar and MacConkey agar. Bacterial isolates were further processed for checking their colony characteristics using microscopy (Gram stain) and basic biochemical reactions. Colonies yielding Gram-positive cocci (GPC) were subjected to the Catalase test. Further coagulase test (Slide and tube method) was performed on all catalase-positive GPC in clusters to further differentiate them as Staphylococcus aureus or Coagulase-Negative Staphylococci (CONS). A few isolates of CONS were processed using VITEK-2 to confirm biochemical test findings. All coagulase test-positive isolates were further identified and tested for antimicrobial susceptibility to confirm whether they were Methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus (MRSA) or Methicillin-sensitive Staphylococcus aureus. (MSSA) strains. Spore-bearing Gram-positive bacilli were only subjected to basic microscopy, as isolates cannot be identified by VITEK-2 compact.\n\n### 2.2 Statistical analysis\n\nThe data collected using Epicollect and statistical analyses were performed using R software version 4.2.3 [25]. Descriptive statistics were calculated using the mean (SD), Median (IQR), frequencies, and percentages. The outcome variable considered in the study was the number of bacterial colonies in the mask. The average count of bacterial colonies was compared across levels of potential risk factors. For statistical significance, univariate quasi-Poisson regression(suitable for count data) was used, and Relative risk, 95% confidence interval, and related p-values were reported. A further stepwise regression technique was used to choose the best set of predictors, and then a multivariable quasi-Poisson regression was applied. Adjusted Relative risk, 95% confidence interval, and p-values were reported. We considered a statistically significant p-value to be less than 0.05.\n\n## 3 Results\n\nThe study population consisted of 400 HCWs with a mean age of 30.8 ± 9.6 years from different settings, like the ICU, Emergency, OPD, and ward. Among participants, 144 (36%), 123 (30.8%), and 110 (27.5%) were doctors, nursing aids, and nurses, respectively. A small proportion, 5.8%, included DOTS clinic staff, Optometrists, and other staff who were directly involved in patient care. A majority, 267 (66.8%) of the participants included in the study were females. The median work experience among participants was 4 years (IQR 1, 10). The participant’s average working time per day was 8.57 ± 2.43 h. Nearly two-thirds of the participants, 260 (65%), contacted at least 25 patients each day. Except for 4 HCWs (1%), none were using whole body PPE while caring for patients. More than 70% of participants used surgical masks. The practice of hand hygiene was found to be poor among the participants with respect to mask usage. The distribution of participants based on mask usage and hygiene is shown in Table 1.\n\n**Table 1 Distribution of study population based on the pattern of mask usage and hygiene**\n\nFull size table\n\n### 3.1 Bacterial contamination in masks and the working environment\n\nBacterial contamination was observed in 338 (84.5%) of mask samples, with bacterial colonies detected in 297 (74.3%) and 314 (78.5%) in the outer and inner parts of the masks, respectively (Table 2). The number of colonies in contaminated masks ranged from 1 to 60 colonies/mask. More than 1 organism was also identified from a single mask. The most common organisms from the inner portion of masks were CONS 236 (59%), Micrococci 126 (31.5%) and Aerobic spore-bearing Gram-positive bacilli (ASB) 18(4.5%). In addition to that, 7 (1.75%) isolates of Staphylococcus aureus were also identified from the inner side, of which 6 (1.5%) were MRSA, and 1 (0.25%) was MSSA. Among CONS, species like *saprophyticus* and *hemolyticus* were also isolated. Among the bacteria isolated from the outer portion of the mask, 228(57%) were (CONS), 124(31%) Micrococci and 29(7.25%) were Aerobic spore-bearing Gram-positive bacilli. Staphylococcus aureus was not isolated from the outer portion of any mask samples. Antibacterial susceptibility testing was performed for all the Staphylococcus aureus isolates as well as for a few representative CONS using the VITEK GP (Gram-positive cocci) susceptibility panel, in addition to screening for methicillin/cefoxitin resistance. The panel included antibiotics benzylpenicillin, gentamicin, erythromycin, clindamycin, linezolid, vancomycin, teicoplanin, tetracycline, ciprofloxacin/levofloxacin and trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole as per CLSI 2022 guidelines. A total of 20 staphylococcal isolates were tested for antimicrobial susceptibility. These included 6 Methicillin resistant Staphylococcus aureus (MRSA), 3 Methicillin sensitive Staphylococcus aureus (MSSA), 5 Staphylococcus epidermidis, 4 S. haemolyticus, 1 S. hominis and 1 S. saprophyticus. (Details in Supplementary file Table 1) Cefoxitin resistance was seen in all MRSA (6/6) and S. epidermidis (5/5) isolates, while 2/4 S. haemolyticus and 1/1 S. saprophyticus isolates were resistant. Resistance to ciprofloxacin and levofloxacin was noted in 3/6 MRSA and 2/5 S. epidermidis isolates. Erythromycin and clindamycin resistance was also observed, particularly among S. epidermidis isolates. All isolates were susceptible to linezolid, daptomycin and tigecycline. One isolate of S. haemolyticus showed resistance to vancomycin.\n\nAntimicrobial resistance pattern of the isolates of Staphylococcus aureus demonstrated high resistance to β-lactam agents like benzyl penicillin and cefoxitin, indicating a substantial burden of methicillin resistance among the isolates studied. Moderate resistance was observed against erythromycin and fluoroquinolones like ciprofloxacin and levofloxacin. In contrast, lower resistance rates were noted for tetracycline, teicoplanin, vancomycin and cotrimoxazole. Notably, all isolates were susceptible to gentamicin, linezolid, daptomycin, and tigecycline. The same pattern was noted among the CONS isolates tested. The other isolates recovered in the study, which were predominantly Gram-positive bacilli (Bacillus spp.) and Micrococcus spp., are generally considered environmental/commensal organisms with limited clinical significance. Therefore, these isolates were not subjected to detailed AST, as the primary focus was on clinically relevant isolates, including MRSA and methicillin-resistant CoNS. In addition, standardised AST interpretation guidelines and clinically validated breakpoints for environmental/commensal organisms such as Bacillus spp. and Micrococcus spp. are limited and not routinely recommended in the absence of clear pathogenic significance. Interpreting susceptibility results is challenging.\n\nBacterial colonies were detected in all environmental samples collected from the OPD, Wards, Emergency departments, and ICUs. The isolates identified from these environmental samples belonged to the same Genus as those identified from the masks of personnel working there. However, the MRSA strains identified from the inner side of the mask were not present in the working environment of the HCWs (Table 3).\n\n**Table 2 Distribution of bacterial characteristics in the study sample**\n\nFull size table\n\n**Table 3 Distribution of bacterial characteristics across different hospital departments**\n\nFull size table\n\nA one-way ANOVA was performed to determine if there were statistically significant differences in the indoor bacterial load among various hospital departments. The analysis revealed a statistically significant difference (F = 27.361, *p* < 0.001), indicating that the average bacterial load varied significantly across the departments. The highest mean bacterial count was recorded in the Ward, while the lowest was found in the ICU. Post hoc analysis revealed a statistically significant difference between all areas except for pairwise comparison between Ward and OPD (2.88 ± 1.68 vs. 2.85 ± 1.49).\n\n**Table 4 Distribution of the study population based on factors associated with bacterial contamination**\n\nFull size table\n\nUnivariate quasi-Poisson regression analysis revealed that variables such as higher age, gender, clinical setting of work, use of a surgical mask, higher patient contact, working hours, hospital providing masks, and duration of mask usage are associated with higher rates of bacterial contamination (Table 4). While the initial crude unadjusted Relative Risk (RR) for factors like number of patient contact/day, higher age, working hours and duration of mask usage showed a highly significant association (*p* < 0.001), this effect disappeared in multivariable model. In contrast, the type of mask in the clinical setting of work showed a strong, significant association in both the univariate and multivariable analysis. Other predictors, like gender and whether the mask was hospital-provided, were also linked to the contamination rates in multivariable analysis (Table 4). Even though bacterial load was found to be lower in masks of HCWs who have followed mask hygiene practices, this association was not statistically significant. A similar observation was also made among HCWs who don’t smoke. All factors that found to be non significant in univariate analysis is shown in Supplementary file Table 2.\n\n## 4 Discussion\n\nThe accumulation of bacteria on face masks represents an important microbiological concern, as it increases the microbial burden on the mask surface and may allow persistence of diverse bacterial populations.Even though the initial outer mask contamination is localised, incorrect handling can physically transfer these bacteria, compromising the stringent environmental bioburden controls maintained within intensive care units and other sterile hospital units. The current investigation revealed a high proportion of overall bacterial contamination of 84.5% among the examined masks. Although bacterial contamination was detected in 84.5% of mask samples, most isolates belonged to normal skin or environmental flora, with only a small subset comprising clinically significant or nosocomial pathogens that may cause infections in immunocompromised or critically ill individuals. Park et al. [24] reported a higher frequency of microbial contamination among community volunteers, with a contamination prevalence of 99% on the inner surfaces and 94% on the outer surfaces, alongside lower quantitative bacterial counts among female participants [24]. The current study found clinically important bacteria, such as *Staphylococcus aureus*, in 1.75% of the masks, whereas Nightingale et al., isolated a higher frequency (15.9%) of *Staphylococcus aureus* and gram-negative bacteria (GNB) such as *Klebsiella spp*, *Enterobacter sp*, *Escherichia sp* and *Proteus sp* from mask samples. Similarly, Ostrowski et al. [23] confirmed the presence of S. aureus on both internal and external mask surfaces among hospital staff, highlighting masks as potential vectors of cross-contamination. Our study identified MRSA in 1.5% of samples, unlike the U.S. study, where MRSA was absent. Nightingale et al. [22]. Antibiotic resistance among isolates remains an emerging concern. Bitolitoli et al. [2] found high resistance to Ampicillin, Amoxicillin, and Cefotaxime in reusable facemasks, while Ostrowski et al. [23] reported *Staphylococcus aureus* resistance to Erythromycin in 75% of cases. The variability of resistance patterns underscores the need for rational antibiotic use and monitoring.\n\nOrganisms such as CoNS and Micrococcus species, which were identified in this study, form part of the normal skin flora but may behave as opportunistic pathogens in immunocompromised individuals, with their clinical relevance largely determined by the context of isolation, for example, from ICU settings. Therefore, the detection of these organisms on masks should be interpreted as an indicator of microbial load and hygiene practices rather than direct evidence of pathogenic risk to HCWs or patients. The small proportion of masks contaminated with MRSA and MSSA on the inner surface is noteworthy, as this finding is consistent with the possibility of nasal carriage among healthcare workers, although carriage was not directly assessed in the present study. However, even in these cases, the presence of mask surfaces does not equate to infection without correlating with the clinical data. The other reports similarly identify high proportions of Micrococcus and Staphylococcus species in health facilities [28, 32]. The predominance of organisms such as aerobic spore-bearing Bacillus species on the mask’s inner layer underscores the need for stringent hygiene practices and timely mask replacement [9, 13, 22, 34].\n\nZhiqing et al. [34] noted increased contamination with extended surgical use, and Kelkar et al. [13] suggested efficacy loss after two hours. Shimamura et al. [27] documented a rise in bacterial CFUs on reusable facemasks from hours two to six. While our study showed greater contamination with prolonged use, multivariable analysis revealed no statistically significant correlation, similar to Nightingale et al. [22]. Environmental factors such as artificial saliva and perspiration enhance bacterial survival on mask surfaces, reinforcing the necessity of regular replacement and proper material selection, particularly in humid climates. Park et al. [24]. While limited studies have assessed hand hygiene behaviours, most have not examined their direct relationship with mask contamination. Kamble et al. [11]. Mask material significantly influences bacterial retention by maintaining higher moisture and fostering microbial growth. N95 masks provide a tighter seal and higher filtration efficiency, reducing the risk of both bacterial and viral contamination compared to surgical/cotton masks, which is in line with this study’s findings [4, 17, 27]. Hospitals that provide masks often have protocols, training, and reminders, which increase correct mask usage and compliance among HCWs. When individuals buy their own masks, there is a higher chance of extended use, reuse, or improper cleaning due to cost or limited access.Female gender appeared to be associated with lower bacterial contamination, suggesting a potentially protective effect in this setting. This may be related to generally better adherence to non-pharmaceutical preventive behaviors among women, as previous meta-analytic evidence has shown that women are about 50% more likely than men to practice measures such as handwashing [20]. However, this observed relationship should be interpreted cautiously, as the study did not assess behavioral or exposure-related factors that may explain this finding.\n\nOur study revealed that bacterial counts were highest in emergency departments and wards, with the lowest in ICUs. Our bacterial counts were lower than previously reported [1, 6, 7]. This discrepancy may stem from differences in sampling methods (active & passive), unstandardized reporting units, environmental variables (temperature and humidity), and local infection-control protocols. While passive sampling depends on contaminants being deposited by gravity, active air sampling uses mechanical devices to draw a predetermined volume of air over collection surfaces. According to Karigoudar et al. [12], passive sampling is an excellent, economical, and user-friendly technique for air monitoring in clinical settings [12]. Lower ICU counts likely reflect enhanced ventilation, stringent hygiene, and better staff-to-patient ratios, which are consistent with reports showing reduced ICU contamination during the COVID-19 period [10]. Conversely, crowded and high-turnover areas like Emergency Units exhibited higher loads, probably due to time-sensitive procedures that compromise adherence to infection-control protocols. Previous studies have shown inconsistent contamination patterns across hospital areas. Gülsoy and Karagozoglu [8] and Munyeshyaka et al. [21] observed higher ICU contamination, contrasting our findings. largely due to the presence of critically ill patients and invasive procedures. Our results demonstrate departmental variation in bacterial load, reflecting differences in workflow, patient contact, and environmental control. This highlights the need for customised infection-prevention strategies focusing on high-traffic zones.\n\nMost of the similar studies done in India or abroad have not considered the mask hygiene practices while exploring the associated factors of bacterial contamination in masks, even though they have assessed hygiene practices. By sampling from multiple hospitals rather than a single institution, this study improves representativeness and external validity. However, the scope of this cross-sectional study was confined to microbiological assessment of mask contamination and associated risk factors, without evaluating person-to-person microbial transfer or the health consequences of such contamination for HCWs and patients. The finding of MRSA only on the inner surface of some masks suggests the possibility of endogenous sources, such as nasal colonisation, but this cross-sectional study was not designed to confirm MRSA carriage among HCWs. Therefore, we have reported individual results to the workers concerned and recommended that they be appropriately screened for nasal MRSA colonisation through the institutional infection control services. We recommend longitudinal research studies which explores these aspects, which include infection risks and health outcomes. Integrating regular training to promote mask hygiene awareness can reduce contamination. Periodic surveillance of mask contamination, combined with strict replacement policies and education on safe handling, remains essential in infection prevention frameworks.\n\n## 5 Conclusion\n\nThe high prevalence of bacterial contamination, including potentially harmful and multidrug-resistant organisms on masks used by HCWs, emphasises the need for strict mask handling protocols, regular replacement, and further research into the dynamics of bacterial colonisation and transmission risk.\n\n## Data availability\n\nThe study’s data are not publicly available, however the corresponding author may make them available upon reasonable request.\n\n## Abbreviations\n\n**DOTS:**:   Directly observed treatment, short-course (for TB control)\n**OPD:**:   Outpatient department\n**ICU:**:   Intensive care unit\n**MRSA:**:   Methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus\n**CLSI:**:   Clinical and laboratory standards institute\n**HCWs:**:   Healthcare workers\n**VITEXK:**:   Life technology\n**CONS:**:   Coagulase-negative staphylococci\n**GPC:**:   Gram-positive cocci\n**ASB:**:   Aerobic spore-bearing gram-positive bacilli\n**CFU:**:   Colony forming units\n\n## References\n\n1. Atalay YA, Mengistie E, Tolcha A et al. Indoor air bacterial load and antibiotic susceptibility pattern of isolates at Adare General Hospital in Hawassa, Ethiopia. Front Public Health. 2023; 11:1194850. https://doi.org/10.3389/fpubh\n2. Bitolitoli EN, Mutai MJ, Njeru EM, et al. Bacteria Contamination and Antibiotic Resistance Profile on Worn Reusable Facemasks in Githurai Market, Kenya. Int J Food Sci Biotechnol. 2025;10(2):33–48. https://doi.org/10.11648/j.ijfsb.20251002.12.\n\n   Article \n   Google Scholar\n3. Centres for Disease Control and Prevention. Implementation of personal protective equipment (PPE) use in nursing homes to prevent spread of multidrug-resistant organisms (MDROs). Updated 2024. Accessed 11 Aug . 2025. https://www.cdc.gov/long-term-care-facilities/hc